fork()
-----
fork() is a system call 
pid_t fork(void);

fork - creates a new process by duplicating the calling process. The new process is referred to as the child process. Teh calling process is referred to as the parent process. 

	refer p3.c

	then 

	refer p4.c 

	
fork return type is  pid_t (typedefined integer) 

	On sucess fork() returns : the PID of the child proccess is returned in the parent and 0 is returned in the child. On failure, -1 is returned in the parent, no child process is created. 

	refer p5.c

	

Common cases where fork() fail and return -1
	
	* Process limit exceeded (EAGAIN)

		the user has reached the maximum no.of processes allowed 
		system-wide process limit is exceeded
	
	* Insufficient memory 

		not enought physical memory or virtual memory 
		kernel cannot allocate 
	
			* PCB
			* kernel stack 
			* page tables 

	* Kernel resourc exhaustion 

		Lack of kernel resources such as 

			* PID structures
			* Task structures 
			* kernel stacks 

	* Overcommit restrictions

		if memory overcommit is disabled or restricted 

What happens when fork() fails ?

	* fork() return -1
	* errno is set
	* parent continues execution 
	* child process is not created 

						


